# {{ project_name }}

![CI](https://github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ package_name }}/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)

Generated from [webstack-agent-harness](https://github.com/MrJuancho/webstack-agent-harness)
— a fail-closed engineering harness for software built by autonomous coding agents. It
doesn't ship a product on its own — it ships the guardrails: 8 verification gates, 4
layers of enforcement, and a tiered `just` command set, so an agent can iterate fast
without ever being able to quietly fake a passing state.

If you're an agent working in this repo, read [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md) first — it's
the operational protocol you're expected to follow, and it points to
[`docs/progress.md`](./docs/progress.md) (what the last session left, overwritten each
session) so you don't have to re-derive state from scratch. Humans should read
[`.specify/memory/constitution.md`](./.specify/memory/constitution.md) for the *why*
behind the rules, [`docs/adr/`](./docs/adr/) for specific decisions and their rationale,
and the full spec at [`docs/spec.html`](./docs/spec.html) (also available as a
[PDF](./docs/spec.pdf)) for the complete reference — generated by
`scripts/generate-pdf.sh`, re-run it after any change to gates, tiers, or enforcement
layers so the spec never drifts from the code.

## Quick start

```bash
just setup
```

This bootstraps a fresh clone end-to-end: derives a worktree-local Postgres port and
Docker Compose project name (`scripts/worktree-env.sh`, see below), checks required
tooling (`just doctor`), installs dependencies, installs the git pre-commit hook, resets
the dev database, and runs the Level 2 gauntlet to confirm everything is green.

Requires: Node.js 22+, pnpm, Docker, `just`, Gitleaks, `jq`, `curl`, `pg_isready`
(postgresql-client — `just db-up` uses it to wait for Postgres for real, from the host).

## Running parallel worktrees

Two `git worktree`s of this same generated project can run `just gauntlet` at the same
time without interfering with each other — no shared state file, no manual port
bookkeeping. `scripts/worktree-env.sh` derives, from the worktree's own path
(`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`), a `PG_PORT` and a `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` unique to
that worktree, and writes them to `.env.local` (gitignored). `just setup` regenerates it
automatically; `just` loads it for every recipe (`set dotenv-filename := ".env.local"`),
so `docker compose up`/`down` and every gate script pick up the right project without
any `-p` flag. The TypeScript side (`drizzle.config.ts`, `src/infra/db/client.ts`, the
migrate/seed scripts) reads `.env.local` directly too, so a direct `pnpm run db:migrate`
or `vitest run` outside of `just` still targets the right instance.

Each worktree's Postgres runs in its own Docker Compose project — separate containers,
network, and host port — so `docker compose down` in one worktree never touches
another's. `just doctor` fails closed if `PG_PORT` is occupied by something that isn't
this worktree's own container, naming the port and pointing at the `PG_PORT=<port>`
environment-variable escape hatch (set it before running `worktree-env.sh` to force a
specific value instead of the derived one).

`just doctor` also checks `.copier-answers.yml`'s `_commit` against the template's real
remote `HEAD` and warns (not fails, by default — being pinned behind `HEAD` is often
intentional) if this project is behind. Set `DOCTOR_STRICT_TEMPLATE_FRESHNESS=1` to make
that a hard failure instead — useful in CI if you want to guarantee a fresh generation.
This exists because a confusing Docker failure with zero indication that the project
came from a stale template snapshot is a real incident, not a hypothetical one.

## The verification tiers

| Tier | Command | When | Scope |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `just gauntlet-fast` | after every file edit | typecheck, lint (incl. domain determinism), unit tests, domain suite (`just test-domain`, no Postgres), suppression justification, staged-secret scan |
| 2 | `just gauntlet` | before every commit | architecture isolation, schema drift, reversible migrations, auth matrix, N+1 detection |
| 3 | `just gauntlet-full` | before merge/PR | property tests, OpenAPI contract fuzzing, seed determinism, hold-out integrity, diff mutation, production image check |
| 4 | `just audit` | scheduled | full mutation testing of `src/domain/**` |

Each tier includes everything in the tier below it. Details on all 10 gates live in
[`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md#2-los-10-gates-de-seguridad).

## Layers and the reference feature

```
domain  <-  application  <-  infra, http        (composition.ts wires them)
```

Each layer imports only from the ones to its left; `infra` and `http` never import each
other. `dependency-cruiser` enforces this in `just gauntlet`. The project ships with one
small **reference feature**, `tasks` (`POST` and `GET /api/v1/tasks`), that crosses every
layer — copy its shape for new features, and delete it once the project has its own. See
[`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md#convención-de-capas) for the file-by-file breakdown.

## Authentication

Protected routes verify a real signed JWT (HS256): the signature, the expiry (`exp`),
and a subject (`sub`) are all required. The server **refuses to start** without a
`JWT_SECRET` of at least 32 characters, and there is no default in the code.

- **Locally**, `scripts/worktree-env.sh` (run by `just setup`) generates a random secret
  per worktree in `.env.local`, which is gitignored.
- **In production**, set `JWT_SECRET` in the deployment environment.
- `just dev-token` prints a 1-hour token signed with the local secret, for manual testing:
  `curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $(just dev-token)" localhost:3000/api/v1/tasks`.

The project verifies tokens; it does not issue them. Login belongs to your identity
provider. To verify against one (JWKS/OIDC), replace the adapter in
`src/infra/auth/jwt-verifier.ts` — routes only depend on the `TokenVerifier` port.

## Running in production

```bash
docker build -t {{ package_name }} .
docker run -e JWT_SECRET=... -e DATABASE_URL=postgres://... -p 3000:3000 {{ package_name }}
```

- **Configuration is validated at startup.** Every environment variable is read once, in
  `src/infra/config.ts`. If anything is missing or invalid, the process exits with code 1
  and lists every problem at once. See `.env.example` for the variables. With
  `NODE_ENV=production` (set in the image), `JWT_SECRET` and `DATABASE_URL` are required.
- **Migrations are a deploy step**, not part of startup:
  `docker run -e DATABASE_URL=... {{ package_name }} node dist/infra/db/migrate.js`.
- **Logs** are JSON on stdout (pino), at `LOG_LEVEL` (default `info`).
- **Graceful shutdown.** On `SIGTERM` or `SIGINT` the server stops accepting connections,
  waits for in-flight requests, closes the database pool, and exits with code 0. If that
  takes more than 10 seconds it exits with code 1.
- **The image runs as a non-root user** and contains only compiled JavaScript and
  production dependencies.

`just image-check` (part of `just gauntlet-full`) builds the image and tests all of the
above against a running container.

## Dependency updates

Dependabot (`.github/dependabot.yml`) opens weekly pull requests for npm packages,
GitHub Actions, the `Dockerfile` base image and the Postgres image in
`docker-compose.yml`. Minor and patch updates arrive grouped in one PR; each major
update gets its own. CI runs `just gauntlet-full` on every one, so a green PR has
passed every gate. Merging is still your decision.

Not proposed automatically: Node and Postgres major versions. Not covered at all
(update by hand): the Gitleaks version pinned in `ci.yml`, the `packageManager` field
in `package.json`, and the Schemathesis image in `scripts/test-contracts.sh`.

## Mutation testing scope

Stryker mutates **only `src/domain/**`** — pure business logic, no I/O, no database, no
clock, no Fastify dependency. That's an enforced convention, not a suggestion: business
logic that leaks into an HTTP handler or an infra adapter falls out of mutation scope
without any gate complaining, which is exactly the failure mode this boundary exists to
prevent. `dependency-cruiser`'s `domain-isolation` rule blocks `src/domain` from
importing any other layer; keeping the *logic* itself there too is on the author and the
reviewer. An ESLint rule (Gate 10, `no-restricted-syntax`, runs in `gauntlet-fast`)
blocks reading the wall clock or a non-deterministic random source from inside
`src/domain` — a single intermittent read there breaks fast-check's reproducibility and
makes Stryker's mutants run differently between runs, the worst possible failure mode
because it passes green most of the time.

Use cases, handlers, adapters, and wiring (`src/application/`, `src/http/`,
`src/infra/`, `src/composition.ts`) are deliberately **not** mutated — not because they don't matter, but because they're already covered more
cheaply and more directly by other gates: Schemathesis audits the OpenAPI contract
(Gate 3), the auth matrix confirms every route declares `preHandler`/`isPublic`
(Gate 4), and the N+1 detector (Gate 6) audits query patterns. Mutating those layers
too would also mean every mutant pays a round trip to Postgres — high cost, low signal,
for a bug class those other gates already catch more directly.

Per-mutant tests run against `vitest.config.domain.ts` (`src/domain/**/*.test.ts` only,
no `globalSetup`, no database) with `coverageAnalysis: 'perTest'`, so each mutant runs
only the tests that actually touch the mutated line — see `just test-domain`. Stryker's
`incremental: true` (state in `.stryker-tmp/`, gitignored) only helps locally: CI starts
from a clean checkout every run, so `just gauntlet-full`/`just audit` in CI always run
in full. See [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md#mutación-qué-se-prueba-y-qué-no) for the full
rationale, including why a `// Stryker disable` or `// eslint-disable ...
no-restricted-syntax` suppression needs a same-line reason (Gate 9) and gets reviewed
individually, not rubber-stamped.

## Enforcement, not just checks

A gate only matters if an agent can't route around it. Four independent layers make
sure of that:

1. **Agent hooks** (`.claude/settings.json`) — block writes to the hold-out test suite,
   block ending a turn on a red `gauntlet-fast`, and give fast (non-blocking) lint
   feedback per edited file. The two security hooks fail *closed*; the feedback hook
   fails *open* — see `AGENTS.md` for the classification and why it matters.
2. **Git pre-commit** (`scripts/hooks/pre-commit.sh`, installed via `just install-hooks`)
   — runs `gauntlet-fast` before any local commit.
3. **CI** (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) — scans the full git history for secrets
   (`gitleaks git`), then runs `just doctor` + `just gauntlet-full` on a clean container
   for every push/PR to `main`.
4. **Branch protection** — `main` requires the CI check green before merge.

## Spec-driven development

This repo uses [spec-kit](https://github.com/github/spec-kit) to plan features before
an agent implements them, so intent survives a dead session or a switch between agents
— write it to a file, not just to chat. Available skills:

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `/speckit-constitution` | Establish or amend project principles |
| `/speckit-specify` | Write a baseline feature specification |
| `/speckit-clarify` | *(optional)* De-risk ambiguous areas before planning |
| `/speckit-plan` | Turn a spec into an implementation plan |
| `/speckit-tasks` | Break a plan into actionable tasks |
| `/speckit-analyze` | *(optional)* Cross-check spec/plan/tasks for consistency |
| `/speckit-implement` | Execute the planned tasks |
| `/speckit-converge` | Assess the codebase and append remaining work as tasks |

The gauntlet still enforces *how well* the result is built; spec-kit just makes sure
*what to build* isn't only ever said out loud.

## Project structure

```
.
├── .claude/
│   ├── agents/               # generator.md, reviewer.md — Claude Code subagents
│   └── settings.json         # Agent hooks (Layer 1)
├── .specify/                 # Spec-kit templates, scripts, and the constitution
├── docs/
│   ├── adr/                  # Architecture decisions: context + consequences
│   └── progress.md           # Session handoff — overwritten, not accumulated
├── scripts/
│   ├── doctor.sh            # Fail-closed tooling check
│   ├── worktree-env.sh      # Derives PG_PORT/COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME into .env.local
│   ├── hooks/                # Agent + git hooks (Layers 1-2)
│   └── test-*.sh             # Individual gate implementations
├── src/
│   ├── domain/               # Pure business rules + co-located *.test.ts (mutated by Stryker)
│   ├── application/          # Use cases + the ports they need (orchestrate, don't decide)
│   ├── infra/db/             # Drizzle schema, migrations, client, repositories (implement ports)
│   ├── http/                  # Fastify app + routes (translate HTTP <-> use cases)
│   ├── composition.ts        # Composition root: wires ports to real adapters
│   └── main.ts               # Process entry point: validates config, then loads the app
├── tests/
│   ├── architecture/          # Gate 4: auth matrix
│   ├── holdout/                # Gate 5: sealed security invariants
│   ├── integration/            # Gate 6: N+1 detection
│   ├── property/                 # fast-check property tests
│   └── unit/
├── vitest.config.ts            # Full suite (tests/**)
├── vitest.config.domain.ts     # Domain-only suite (src/domain/**/*.test.ts), no Postgres
└── justfile                    # Task orchestration for all tiers
```

## Roles for concurrent work

Codified as Claude Code subagents in `.claude/agents/`:

- **Generator** (`generator.md`) — the only role with `Edit`/`Write`. Works at the repo
  root on a feature branch, running `gauntlet-fast` continuously.
- **Reviewer** (`reviewer.md`) — no `Edit`/`Write`; reviews a diff with a clean context
  (never the conversation that produced it). Never inspects code in a tree another
  process might be modifying — uses `just review-start <branch>` for an isolated git
  worktree and `just review-clean` to tear it down. This is plain `git worktree`, not
  tied to any particular IDE or agent-orchestration tool.

See [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md#4-roles-operativos-y-aislamiento-con-git-worktrees) for
the full protocol.
